{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nOperators need an authoritative configuration precedence/source/lifecycle reference and a security reference that stay byte-current with the config schema and the ownership/secret-name registries rather than drifting by hand. The existing `docs/config.md` is a 648-line HAND-AUTHORED operator doc (rationale, precedence narrative, a `## Config-key inventory`, a `### Secrets` list, the `## ticket.default_assignee` section, a fail-loud tombstone-registry section, and stable anchor slugs) with many in-repo consumers: content-asserting tests (`tests/unit/test_snapshot_docs.py`, `test_session_id.py`, `test_structured_run.py`, `test_harness_provenance.py`, `test_session_shims.py`, `test_agents_claim_assignee_guidance.py`, `test_build_drift.py`, `test_llm_model_env_removal.py`), anchor-fragment deep links (`AGENTS.md#ticketdefault_assignee`, `docs/reuse-surface.md#rebar_operation_snapshot_shadow`, `docs/concurrency.md#config-key-inventory`), and source-guidance references (`src/rebar/_config_coercion.py`, `_config_schema.py`, `_operation_config.py`, `llm/config.py`). Byte-generating into `config.md` would strand these consumers and duplicate its hand-authored inventory.\n\n## Outcome\nA new `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` generates a NEW standalone, fully-generated `docs/config-reference.md` (the machine-current, registry-derived configuration precedence/source/lifecycle reference) and a NEW indexed `docs/security.md`, both from the config schema plus the ownership and secret-name registries, with per-key LIFECYCLE (deprecation/removal status) derived from the `kind==\"cfg\"` entries of `rebar._deprecations.REGISTRY` plus `rebar._deprecations.tombstones()` filtered to `kind==\"cfg\"` — a NEW cfg-channel derivation modelled on how `gen_env_registry.py` reads `kind==\"env\"` deprecation entries (NOT an existing tombstone read in that generator; the typed schema itself carries no lifecycle). The existing hand-authored `docs/config.md` is LEFT ENTIRELY UNCHANGED — it remains the rationale/narrative doc, so every one of its consumers (content-asserting tests, anchor deep links, source references) stays intact by construction. Non-registry-derivable narrative topics named by the epic (observability exception, subprocess limitations, explicit behavior deltas, native refresh) are supplied VERBATIM from a curated template constant embedded in the generator, not synthesized; registry-derived tables (per-key precedence/source/lifecycle, secret-name inventory) are computed from the schema + ownership/secret-name registries + the `kind==\"cfg\"` entries of `rebar._deprecations.REGISTRY` and `rebar._deprecations.tombstones()` (which supply the lifecycle column). `docs/README.md` and `docs/architecture.md` navigation reference the new docs. A newline-normalizing drift check (portable, run under `make lint`, no CI dependency) fails when either generated doc is stale.\n\n## Plan\n1. Add `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` (modelled on `gen_env_registry.py`/`gen_cli_reference.py`): read the config schema + ownership + secret-name registries + the `kind==\"cfg\"` entries of `rebar._deprecations.REGISTRY` and `rebar._deprecations.tombstones()` (the per-key lifecycle/deprecation/removal source — a new cfg-channel derivation modelled on how `gen_env_registry.py` reads `kind==\"env\"` entries) and render (a) registry-derived tables and (b) the curated template constant (holding the observability/subprocess/behavior-delta/native-refresh narrative verbatim) into a fully-generated `docs/config-reference.md` and into `docs/security.md`. Both files are generated whole (no partial-region markers needed — they are new, generator-owned files). Each generated doc carries a top-of-file markdown banner within its first 8 lines that contains the word \"generated\" AND the exact regenerate command string `python scripts/gen_config_reference.py`, as required by the generated-surface parity gate `tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py::test_text_surface_carries_a_banner`.\n2. Create `docs/config-reference.md` and `docs/security.md`; update `docs/README.md` and `docs/architecture.md` navigation to link them. Register BOTH new docs in the generated-surface parity gate so it does not fail: add `docs/config-reference.md` and `docs/security.md` (each → `python scripts/gen_config_reference.py`) to the `GENERATED` map in `tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py`, AND add a matching row for each to the `### Generated — never edit these by hand` TABLE in `docs/README.md` (source description + regenerate command + drift-gate name) — the table row set must EQUAL the `GENERATED`+`GATED_HAND_AUTHORED` registry (`test_readme_table_matches_registry`), so the prose navigation link is not sufficient. Do NOT modify `docs/config.md`.\n3. Give `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` a `--check` mode (modelled on the existing `gen_env_registry.py --check` doc-drift gate already run under `make lint`) and wire `python scripts/gen_config_reference.py --check` into `make lint` (portable/no-CI path): regenerate in-memory and compare to the checked-out files with newline normalization (universal-newline / text-mode read, not a raw byte compare) so a CRLF autocrlf checkout on Windows does not fail spuriously. The drift check lives on the generator itself (a `--check` flag), not a separate module, exactly as the cited prior art does.\n\n## Testing (RED -> GREEN)\n- Drift: a deliberate edit to either generated file trips the drift check; regenerating clears it.\n- Preservation (by construction): `docs/config.md` is byte-unchanged, proven by a test asserting the generator/drift-check never write `config.md`; the existing config.md content-asserting suites named in Context continue to pass.\n- Topic coverage (concrete proving command): a test asserts that, across `docs/config-reference.md` and `docs/security.md` together, a heading/anchor exists for every entry in an explicit REQUIRED_TOPICS list (each topic routed to its owning doc — security topics to security.md, config topics to config-reference.md) (config precedence, source, lifecycle, restart/redeploy rotation, native refresh, observability exception, subprocess limitations, behavior deltas, MCP/CLI exposure), failing if any is missing.\n- Newline safety: a regression test feeds CRLF-encoded checked-out content while the generator emits LF and asserts the drift check reports NO drift.\n- Parity gate: `tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py` passes — each new doc carries its self-announcing banner (`test_text_surface_carries_a_banner`) and the `docs/README.md` table row set equals the registry (`test_readme_table_matches_registry`).\n- Portability: the generator and `--check` drift gate run under `make lint` with no CI provider present.\n\n## Compatibility / rollback\nDocs-and-tooling only; no product-behavior change. `docs/config.md` and all its consumers are untouched. Rollback removes the generator (with its `--check` mode), the curated template constant, `docs/config-reference.md`, `docs/security.md`, its `make lint` wiring, the two `GENERATED`-map/README-table registrations, and reverts the two navigation edits.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` generates `docs/config-reference.md` and `docs/security.md` whole from the config schema + ownership + secret-name registries plus a curated template constant, and never writes `docs/config.md`.\n- [ ] `docs/config.md` is byte-unchanged and all its enumerated consumers remain intact (its content-asserting suites pass; its anchor slugs still resolve), proven by a test that the generator/drift-check do not target `config.md`.\n- [ ] across `docs/config-reference.md` and `docs/security.md`, an anchor exists for every topic in the REQUIRED_TOPICS list (each routed to its owning doc) (precedence, source, lifecycle, restart/redeploy rotation, native refresh, observability exception, subprocess limitations, behavior deltas, MCP/CLI exposure), proven by a topic-coverage test.\n- [ ] `docs/config-reference.md` carries a top-of-file banner (within its first 8 lines) that names `python scripts/gen_config_reference.py`, and `docs/security.md` carries the same, satisfying `test_generated_surface_markers.py::test_text_surface_carries_a_banner`.\n- [ ] Both new docs are registered in the `GENERATED` map of `tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py` and in the `docs/README.md` `### Generated — never edit these by hand` table, so `test_readme_table_matches_registry` passes.\n- [ ] The `python scripts/gen_config_reference.py --check` drift gate runs under `make lint` with no CI dependency, fails on a stale generated doc, passes after regeneration, and does NOT false-fail on a CRLF checkout (newline-normalized comparison).\n- [ ] `docs/README.md` and `docs/architecture.md` navigation reference the new docs."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787151976268532001,"uuid":"d4f020c4-445c-4083-808c-255ff7bffbb9"}